      "x": 2530,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 460,
      "text": "A \u00b7 Sticker Shop\nThick ink outlines, hard offset shadows, flat bold colors on warm cream. Lilita One + Nunito."
    },
    {
      "id": "direction-b",
      "page": "style-options",
      "x": 2530,
      "y": 1320,
      "w": 460,
      "text": "B \u00b7 Saturday Morning\nDeep grape stage, candy-colored bubbles, no outlines. Titan One + Baloo 2."
    },
    {
      "id": "no-tv-notes",
      "page": "no-tv",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1180,
      "w": 1100,
      "text": "No-TV mode: no shared screen anywhere. Every phone carries the shared stage on top and that player's own controls below.\n\nTop row is the platform: starting a room from a phone, the room code read aloud, and a game that needs a TV shown disabled rather than hidden.\nSecond row is Imposter in phase order. Word check deliberately shows an empty stage \u2014 a phase shows the stage or your secret, never both.\n\nPhones are silent here, haptics only, so the Suspense ring on Reveal and Last chance is the visual standing in for the drumroll.\nAll names, the room code and the words are sample content."
    },
    {
      "id": "doodle-bluff-notes",
      "page": "doodle-bluff",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1240,
      "w": 1100,
      "text": "Doodle Bluff. Everyone draws two secret prompts up front, then drawings are shown one at a time: everyone but the artist writes a fake title, then the room votes for the real one.\n\nThe six marker inks are settled here, contrast-checked against the paper. Colour is decoration only \u2014 no rule, score, title or vote ever reads a stroke's colour.\n\nThe gallery is the finale: every drawing made in the game, including the ones never titled, and then they die with the room.\nAll names, prompts and titles are sample content."
    },
    {
      "id": "sketch-phone-notes",
      "page": "sketch-phone",
      "x": 3500,
      "y": 1300,
      "w": 1100,
      "text": "Sketch Phone, proposed for issue #145. Nothing here is built yet. Every player starts a chain with a line of words. Each round the chains move one seat on, and the rounds take turns: draw what you got, then describe the drawing you got. At the end every chain plays back, one link at a time.\n\nProposals for the open questions (the plan settles them):\n- Chain length: the player count, rounded down to an odd number and capped at 7 (3 or 4 players: 3 links; 8 players: 7). A chain always starts and ends with words, so the end can be read against the start. At 8 players one player sits out each chain; Maya isn't in Priya's.\n- Times: write 45 s, draw 75 s, describe 40 s. About 6 minutes of play at 8 players, then about 70 s of replay and vote per chain.\n- The chain's starter presents it: their phone holds Next, and the link moves on by itself after 10 s. Phones that aren't presenting show the kit's Look up screen while there's a TV.\n- Scoring: after each chain, everyone picks a favourite link that isn't their own. Each vote is +100 for that link's author; ties share. Final scores and the crown use the platform's existing screens, so nothing new is drawn for them.\n- Words are capped at 50 characters; 'Give me one' fills the first line from the drawing-prompts pack.\n- Time runs out: a drawing goes on as it is. An empty line is replaced from the pack and tagged 'Out of time' in the replay.\n- No shared screen: the game opts in. Each phone carries the replay as its stage, with the presenter's Next button or a line saying who has it below.\n\nRows: the TV; phones with a TV; phones alone; Hebrew, right to left; the 360 and 430 wide phones with the longest line (50 characters) and 12-character names.\nAll names, lines and drawings are sample content."
    }
  ],
